# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# Genesis Controls build overlay. Loaded LAST, after every other defaults file:
#
#   idf.py -C firmware -B firmware/build-dev-s3-psram \
#       -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.defaults.revb;sdkconfig.defaults.dev-s3-psram;sdkconfig.defaults.genesis" \
#       -DUPSA_IMAGE_NAME=ups-adaptor-dev-s3-psram build
#
# ESP-IDF applies SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS in order and later files win, so this must
# come last or it does nothing. It also reads SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS only when it
# GENERATES sdkconfig: with a generated sdkconfig already in the build
# directory, editing this file changes nothing until that sdkconfig is deleted.
# Upstream guards the same hazard in .github/workflows/ci.yml with
# `rm -f firmware/sdkconfig`.
#
# Nothing here edits an upstream file. Everything is an override, so rebasing on
# aronreid/ups-to-esp32 stays clean.

# --- Fleet check-in: off ------------------------------------------------------
#
# sdkconfig.defaults ships a live endpoint and publishable key for the upstream
# project's check-in, and fleet.c defaults the heartbeat to ON. It gates all
# sending on both strings being non-empty:
#
#     s_st.configured = CONFIG_UPSA_FLEET_URL[0] && CONFIG_UPSA_FLEET_KEY[0];
#
# Emptying them leaves the component compiled in but inert: the task starts,
# finds itself unconfigured, sends nothing, and the status page says so.
#
# Why Genesis sets this. These adapters are installed at roughly 20 CUSTOMER
# sites. Left at the upstream default, each board would report to a third-party
# database every 6 hours with its MAC address, firmware version, board name,
# uptime and reset reason (telemetry/supabase.sql). A MAC address identifies a
# specific unit on a specific customer's site, and that is Genesis's disclosure
# to make deliberately rather than inherit.
#
# This is not a criticism of upstream. The key it ships is a PUBLISHABLE (anon)
# key scoped to the heartbeat RPC alone, the feature is opt-out in the web UI,
# and fleet-wide crash and version data is exactly how that project finds the
# UPS models its parser cannot read.
#
# Verify after every build -- this is cheap and it is how the default was caught
# in the first place:
#
#   grep -a supabase.co firmware/build-<target>/ups-adaptor-<target>.bin
#
# It must print nothing. If it prints a URL, this file was not loaded last, or a
# stale generated sdkconfig was reused.
CONFIG_UPSA_FLEET_URL=""
CONFIG_UPSA_FLEET_KEY=""

# --- Default login -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# auth_init() seeds these into NVS on a board that has none, so an adapter is
# usable the moment it powers up and the setup page does not insist on a login
# before it will do anything. Stored salted and hashed; this file is the only
# plain text.
#
# DELIBERATELY PUBLIC. This fork is public and GPL sends the corresponding
# source to every customer who receives a board, so this password is known to
# anyone who wants it. That is an accepted trade (decided 2026-11-20): these
# adapters are read-only on the bus, the login gates writes to the web UI only,
# and the alternative -- twenty sites with twenty passwords -- costs more in
# practice than it buys.
#
# What it is NOT: a secret. If a site needs one, change the password on that
# board's page; auth_is_default() then goes false and the page stops saying it
# is on the default. The page asks for the change and never requires it.
#
# A per-device default derived from the MAC, printed on a label, would be
# stronger and needs no shared secret. Worth revisiting if these ever go
# somewhere the network is not already trusted.
CONFIG_UPSA_DEFAULT_ADMIN_USER="admin"
CONFIG_UPSA_DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASS="Admin1234"

# --- No guided Wi-Fi setup screen --------------------------------------------
#
# Upstream shows nothing but the Wi-Fi card until the board joins a network,
# and hides the menu, the status and every other card behind it. That is right
# for upstream: its board does nothing useful until it is on a network.
#
# This adapter's output is RS485. Roughly one unit in twenty ever joins a
# Wi-Fi, and the radio exists so a technician can open this page. With the
# guided screen on, every other unit would sit behind a Wi-Fi form for its
# whole service life, unable to reach its own settings -- including the one
# that changes the default password.
#
# Off, the page comes up normally on the board's own access point and Wi-Fi is
# one ordinary card among the others, there for the units that want it.
CONFIG_UPSA_SETUP_SCREEN=n

# --- Setup AP closes after ten minutes ---------------------------------------
#
# Upstream leaves the setup access point up until the board joins a network,
# which for most of these adapters is never. An open, unauthenticated AP
# broadcasting for years in twenty customer buildings is a standing invitation,
# and it is on by default.
#
# Ten minutes from power-up is enough to configure a board. The clock restarts
# whenever a client is associated, so it cannot close under a technician
# mid-configuration, and it restarts on every BOOT press.
#
# The way back is deliberately physical, because it has to work when the
# network is off: press BOOT, or power-cycle the board. A power cycle always
# gives a fresh ten minutes, so there is no state in which a board is
# unreachable to someone standing next to it.
#
# A board that HAS joined a network is never touched by this -- its radio is
# carrying Modbus TCP or BACnet/IP.
CONFIG_UPSA_AP_WINDOW_S=600
